    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Trophic interactions play crucial roles in structuring complex ecosystems. The bat star (Patiria miniata) is an abundant kelp forest echinoderm whose trophic ecology remains poorly understood. Though it has traditionally been considered an omnivorous generalist, most work on Patiria has focused on specific effects of predation on select groups of flora and fauna, with little consideration of its overall role in the kelp forest ecosystem. Understanding Patiria’s trophic role is especially important given the recent declines in many asteroid species due to sea star wasting disease. Patiria was left mostly untouched by the outbreak and could potentially fill the niches left by other ecologically-important sea star species. We used a combination of feeding experiments and comparative stable isotope analysis to examine various aspects of Patiria’s trophic ecology. Contrary to Patiria’s omnivorous reputation, initial feeding experiments revealed a predominantly carnivorous diet with little evidence of herbivory. Perplexed by these results, we conducted additional experiments which suggested that Patiria may feed on the epiphytic communities growing on algae, but not on the algae themselves. Finally, isotopic comparisons of bat stars with giant sea stars (Pisaster giganteus), purple sea urchins (Strongylocentrotus purpuratus), and warty sea cucumbers (Parastichopus parvimensis) revealed that bat stars occupy a high trophic level, have relatively low trophic diversity, and are isotopically most similar to giant sea stars (a carnivorous species). Altogether, these results suggest that Patiria has a narrower niche than previously expected and a trophic role similar to that of carnivorous sea stars like Pisaster.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
